// REJECTS: no viable alternative at input ''first'~message:'
//
// Each constraint clause may appear AT MOST ONCE. The clause sequence has no repetition
// operator at all, so a second ~message is not "the last one wins" and not "override" --
// it is unparseable.
//
// Contrast with exclusive-two-auth-strategies.pure, where the same idea (a field given
// twice) is caught by the WALKER with a named-field message, because the connection rule
// really is an unordered `( ... )*`. Two constructs, two layers, two diagnostics. A rewrite
// that implements both as "parse a bag of key/value clauses" gets the wrong answer twice:
// it accepts this one, and it accepts the connection one too.
###Pure
Class fixture::neg::TwoMessages
[
   c1
   (
      ~function: $this.code->length() > 2
      ~message: 'first'
      ~message: 'second'
   )
]
{
   code: String[1];
}
